Q: How do I prepend file to beginning? A: <p>This has long been an itch of mine - so I decided to scratch it finally and made a convenient function of it. Thoughts?</p>
<pre><code>&lt;?php
    //
    // Prepends some data to a file.
    //
    // @param string $name The filename. If it doesn't exist
    //                     it's created.
    // @param $data string The new data to prepend to the file
    // @param $block number The block size to use when reading
    //                      the original file and also the
    //                      temp file. Default is 1024.
    //
    function file_prepend($name, $data, $block = 1024)
    {
        if (file_exists($name)) {

            // Open both files
            $src = fopen($name, 'r+');
            $tmp = fopen('php://temp/maxmemory:' . $block, 'r+');

            // First add the new data to the start of the tmp file
            fwrite($tmp, $data);

            // Read a block of data from the input file
            while($b = fread($src, $block)) {            

                // Write the block of data to the tmp file
                fwrite($tmp, $b);
            }

            // Now write all of the temporary file descriptor to the
            // file.
            fseek($src, 0, SEEK_SET);
            fseek($tmp, 0, SEEK_SET);

            while($b = fread($tmp, $block)) {            

                // Write the block of data to the tmp file
                fwrite($src, $b);
            }

            fclose($tmp);
            fclose($src);

        } else {
            file_put_contents($name, $data);
        }
    }

    file_prepend('foo.txt', &quot;Some data to prepend\r\n&quot;);
?&gt;
</code></pre>

Q: importing and exporting modules in react A: <p>The problem is in your import. In <code>B.js</code> you’re doing this:</p>
<pre><code>var fun = require('./B.js'); // wrong
</code></pre>
<p>That’s just requiring itself. You want to import <code>A.js</code>:</p>
<pre><code>const fun = require('./A.js');
fun();
</code></pre>
<p>And in <code>A.js</code> just export the function:</p>
<pre><code>function fun() {
  console.log(&quot;Hello from A.js&quot;);
}
module.exports = fun;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>If you’re in a React project, usually you don’t use <code>require</code> but ES modules instead:</p>
<pre><code>export default function fun() {
  console.log(&quot;Hello from A.js&quot;);
}

import fun from './A.js';
fun();
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>That should work in both Node (CommonJS) and React (ES modules), depending on how your project is set up.</p>

Q: Video File Upload Progress Bar with Javascript and PHP A: <p><strong>Here is a ajax function for modern browsers:</strong></p>

<pre><code>//url,callback,type,FormData,uploadFunc,downloadFunc
function ajax(a,b,e,d,f,g,c){
 c=new XMLHttpRequest;
 !f||(c.upload.onprogress=f);
 !g||(c.onprogress=g);
 c.onload=b;
 c.open(e||'get',a);
 c.send(d||null)
}
</code></pre>

<p>more about this function <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/18309057/2450730">https://stackoverflow.com/a/18309057/2450730</a></p>

<p>here is the html</p>

<pre><code>&lt;form&gt;&lt;input type="file" name="file"&gt;&lt;input type="submit" value="GO"&gt;&lt;/form&gt;
&lt;canvas width="64" height="64"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
&lt;canvas width="64" height="64"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>you can add more fields inside the form and you don't need to change anything in the javascript functions. <strong>it always sends the whole form</strong>.</p>

<p>this is the code to make this ajax function work</p>

<pre><code>var canvas,pre;
window.onload=function(){
 canvas=document.getElementsByTagName('canvas');
 pre=document.getElementsByTagName('pre')[0];
 document.forms[0].onsubmit=function(e){
  e.preventDefault();
  ajax('upload.php',rdy,'post',new FormData(this),progressup,progressdown)
 }
}
function progressup(e){
 animate(e.loaded/e.total,canvas[0],'rgba(127,227,127,0.3)')
}
function progressdown(e){
 animate(e.loaded/e.total,canvas[1],'rgba(227,127,127,0.3)')
}
function rdy(e){
 pre.textContent=this.response;
}
</code></pre>

<p>this is the animation that moves the  circular canvas progress bar</p>

<pre><code>function animate(p,C,K){
var c=C.getContext("2d"),
x=C.width/2,
r=x-(x/4),
s=(-90/180)*Math.PI,
p=p||0,
e=(((p*360|0)-90)/180)*Math.PI;
c.clearRect(0,0,C.width,C.height);
c.fillStyle=K;
c.textAlign='center'; 
c.font='bold '+(x/2)+'px Arial';
c.fillText(p*100|0,x,x+(x/5));
c.beginPath();
c.arc(x,x,r,s,e);
c.lineWidth=x/2;
c.strokeStyle=K;
c.stroke();
}
</code></pre>

<p>you can extend this function with some nice bounce effect on initializzation or on progresschange.</p>

